\details{
\strong{Why.} A DA result can hinge on a setting with no right value. The
ANCOM-BC2 authors strongly recommend their pseudocount sensitivity
analysis (Lin & Peddada 2024), and benchmarks find ANCOM-BC2's results
among the least replicable without it (Pelto et al. 2025). Assumptions
about the samples' total microbial load (the "scale") can break the
control of false discoveries in common tools (McGovern et al. 2023; Dos
Santos et al. 2026), and Nixon et al. (2025) recommend testing over a
range of scale uncertainties. A finding that holds over these settings is
firmer than one that holds at a single setting.

\strong{The runs.} The base run is \code{da_test(counts, method, ...)}. Each other
run is the same call with one setting changed (or, with \code{crossed = TRUE},
one combination of settings). Every run uses the base run's \code{seed}, so
that the runs differ by their settings and not by random draws, and its
\code{cpus}; the results are identical for any \code{cpus}. Runs other than the
base run of \code{\link[=ancombc]{ancombc()}} and \code{\link[=ancombc2]{ancombc2()}} are made with \code{sensitivity = FALSE}. Warnings from the runs are given once each, and not when the base
run already gave them.

\strong{What each setting means.}
\itemize{
\item \code{pseudocounts}: what \code{pseudocount} means differs by method. For
\code{\link[=ancom]{ancom()}}, \code{\link[=da_wilcoxon]{da_wilcoxon()}}, \code{\link[=da_ttest]{da_ttest()}} and \code{\link[=da_lm]{da_lm()}} it is added to
every value before the logs, to patch the zeros (for the last three,
only with \code{norm = 'clr'} or \code{'log'}, the norms that take logs). For
\code{\link[=ancombc]{ancombc()}}
and \code{\link[=ancombc2]{ancombc2()}} it is added to every value, as in ANCOMBC's own
sensitivity analysis. For \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}} it is the Dirichlet
prior, added to the counts before the posterior draws.
\item \code{zeros}: replaces the zeros with \code{\link[=replace_zeros]{replace_zeros()}} instead of a
pseudocount, for the methods that take any table of positive values.
\item \code{scale_sds}: \code{\link[=dirmult_ttest]{dirmult_ttest()}}'s scale model, the standard deviation of
the samples' log total load (ALDEx2's gamma): \code{0} assumes the loads are
the same, larger values allow more variation and give wider intervals.
}

\strong{Relation to ANCOMBC's \code{passed_ss}.} \code{summary$passed} is ANCOMBC's
\code{passed_ss} generalised to any method and setting: with \code{method = 'ancombc2'} and \code{pseudocounts = TRUE}, it is exactly \code{\link[=ancombc2]{ancombc2()}}'s
\code{passed_ss} (and \code{robust} its \code{reject_robust}). These pseudocount runs
are the fits behind \code{passed_ss}: when the base run already made them
(\code{sensitivity = TRUE}, \code{\link[=ancombc2]{ancombc2()}}'s default), they are taken from it
rather than fitted again. The ANCOMBC2 vignette's "sensitivity score"
(the proportion of the pseudocount runs whose adjusted p-value is above
\code{alpha}) is \code{1 - prop_reject} over those runs.

\strong{Plots.} \code{plot(x)} draws one of two displays (see
\code{\link[=plot.ecodive_da_sensitivity]{plot.ecodive_da_sensitivity()}}): \code{"tiles"} (the default) shows each
feature's decision (rows) in every run (columns), coloured by the sign of
the effect where the run rejects it, with the features whose decision
never changes in bold; \code{"traces"} shows each feature's effect against one
setting's values, with filled points where the run rejects it (for
\code{scale_sds}, the display of ALDEx2's \code{plotGamma()}).
}
\section{Input Types}{


The \code{counts} parameter is designed to accept a simple numeric matrix, but
seamlessly supports objects from the following biological data packages:
\itemize{
\item \code{phyloseq}
\item \code{rbiom}
\item \code{SummarizedExperiment}
\item \code{TreeSummarizedExperiment}
}

For large datasets, standard matrix operations may be slow. See
\code{vignette('performance')} for details on using optimized formats
(e.g. sparse matrices) and parallel processing.
}

\examples{
    x <- rbind(
      c( 20, 110, 100, 101, 100, 103, 104),
      c( 33, 110, 120, 100, 101, 100, 102),
      c( 12, 110, 100, 110, 100,  50,  90),
      c(202, 201,   9,  10,  10,  11,  11),
      c(200, 202,  10,  10,  13,  10,  10),
      c(203, 201,  14,  10,  10,  13,  12) )
    colnames(x) <- paste0('b', 1:7)
    grp <- rep(c('treatment', 'placebo'), each = 3)

    # Does the t-test's answer depend on the pseudocount?
    res <- da_sensitivity(x, 'da_ttest', grp, pseudocounts = TRUE, cpus = 1)
    res
    attr(res, 'summary')
    plot(res)

    # The Dirichlet-multinomial t-test over scale uncertainties (gamma).
    res <- da_sensitivity(
      x, 'dirmult_ttest', grp, scale_sds = TRUE, n_draws = 32, cpus = 1 )
    plot(res, 'traces')

}
